@page "/handle"

<PageTitle>Handle · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Handle</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        With <code>Handle=".handle"</code> a row can only be dragged by grabbing the handle icon. The rest of the row
        keeps its normal pointer behaviour: its text can be selected, its inputs focused and typed into, its buttons
        clicked.
    </p>
</div>

<DemoCode />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A drag handle</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>Handle</code> is a CSS selector, matched inside each item. Press anywhere else on a row and nothing is
        dragged - select its text instead.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_items" TItem="string" Handle=".handle" Id="handle-list"
                  Class="demo-list" ItemClass="demo-item" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="item">
                <span class="handle">☰</span>@item
            </ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Order:</strong> <span id="handle-order">@string.Join(", ", _items)</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Rows with controls</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Where a handle earns its place: rows that hold controls. Each step of this checklist is an input you can click
        into and edit, with a checkbox to tick - and the grip on the left reorders the steps.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <Bortable @bind-Items="_steps" TItem="Step" Handle=".handle" Id="handle-steps" AriaLabel="Checklist"
                  ItemLabel="s => s.Text"
                  Class="demo-list" ItemClass="demo-item" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
            <ItemTemplate Context="step">
                <span class="demo-row">
                    <span class="handle" title="Drag to reorder">☰</span>
                    <input type="checkbox" @bind="step.Done" aria-label="@($"{step.Text} done")" />
                    <input type="text" @bind="step.Text" @bind:event="oninput" aria-label="Step" />
                </span>
            </ItemTemplate>
        </Bortable>

        <p class="demo-order"><strong>Steps:</strong> <span id="handle-steps-order">@string.Join(", ", _steps.Select(s => s.Done ? $"{s.Text} ✓" : s.Text))</span></p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Drag a row of the first list by its body: nothing moves, and the text can be selected. Drag it by ☰: it sorts.</li>
        <li>
            In the checklist, click into a step and edit it, tick its box, then reorder it by ☰ - the edit and the tick
            travel with it, because they live in the item.
        </li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard nothing changes: <kbd>Tab</kbd> to a row (not to the input inside it) and <kbd>Space</kbd>
            picks it up. Keys pressed inside the input are the input's - <kbd>Space</kbd> types a space.
        </li>
        <li>
            On a touch screen a handle still needs the long press; the <a href="touch">touch</a> page shows a handle that
            drags at once.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> How it works</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            With a <code>Handle</code>, items render as not draggable. Pressing an element that matches the selector
            makes its item draggable for the drag that follows; the next press starts from undraggable again.
        </li>
        <li>
            The list's container gets the <code>bit-bortable-has-handle</code> class, which the stylesheet uses to take
            the grab cursor and the no-select rule off the rest of the item. Give the handle its own grab cursor.
        </li>
        <li>
            A handle belongs to the item it sits in: in <a href="nested">nested lists</a>, a handle of an inner list's
            item never drags the outer item around it.
        </li>
        <li>
            An invalid selector does not break the page: the browser layer warns once in the console, and no item drags.
        </li>
        <li>
            For a handle that a keyboard user reaches as well, make it a <code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;</code> with an
            accessible name: it picks its item up too.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Parameters on this page</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Here</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Handle</code></td><td><code>.handle</code></td><td>The CSS selector of the element, inside each item, that starts a drag.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>ItemLabel</code></td><td><code>s =&gt; s.Text</code></td><td>How announcements name a step: by its text, as it is now.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>AriaLabel</code></td><td><code>Checklist</code></td><td>The list's accessible name.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private IList<string> _items = new List<string>
    {
        "Item 1", "Item 2", "Item 3", "Item 4", "Item 5", "Item 6"
    };

    private IList<Step> _steps = new List<Step>
    {
        new() { Text = "Pack the tent" },
        new() { Text = "Check the weather", Done = true },
        new() { Text = "Fill the water bottles" },
        new() { Text = "Charge the torch" },
    };

    // A class: the item is edited in place, and its @key - the object itself - stays the same while its text changes.
    public sealed class Step
    {
        public required string Text { get; set; }
        public bool Done { get; set; }
    }
}
